id: core/orientation
title: "orientation"
kind: core
status: draft-0.2
updated: 2026-08-27

definition: >
  Orientation is the revisable, attributed process and working state through
  which an Agent locates itself in relationship with an Arena from within a
  partial information field. It composes enough current context, concern,
  uncertainty, authority, affordance, and proof conditions to make inquiry,
  holding, choice, or movement inspectable without pretending to contain the
  Agent, Arena, relationship, or reality.

structural_and_operational_order:
  relationship: "structurally first; it conditions what can become relevant"
  observation: "operationally first; it makes the active relationship inspectable"
  orientation: "revises the working map from attributed observations and inferences"
  movement: "optional; inquiry, presence, holding, waiting, and deliberate non-action remain valid"
  update: "proof and authorized events may revise the map and relationship"

orientation_records:
  relationship_ref: "the active Agent-Arena coupling being oriented within"
  principal_and_role_refs: "whose rights govern and which situated roles are active"
  arena_ref: "the provisional purpose-relevant slice of the world"
  observation_refs: "what became available, from where, and through what access"
  observer_stance: "how the observer is positioned; normally a stance of an existing Agent"
  attention: "what currently has focus and why it may be salient"
  current_location: "where the Agent thinks it is physically, socially, temporally, and structurally"
  purpose_or_governing_question: "what makes this orientation relevant now"
  current_state_refs: "attributed body, relationship, environment, and system state"
  concern_need_value_and_commitment_refs: "what exerts pressure or matters, without converting desire into authority"
  resource_constraint_and_capability_refs: "what enables, limits, or changes available participation"
  authority_boundary_refs: "what must be protected and who may decide, disclose, or act"
  lacuna_and_open_question_refs: "what remains unknown, disputed, missing, or unresolved"
  affordance_or_candidate_move_refs: "what inquiry, holding, participation, or movement appears available"
  proof_and_revision_refs: "what could revise specific claims and which orientation revision is current"

one_model_rule: >
  These records reference the shared YAWN ontology. They do not create a second
  personal orientation ontology, a parallel Agent model, or mandatory storage
  fields for every human interaction.

perspective_boundaries:
  observer: >
    An observer is normally a metacognitive stance, role, or View of an embodied
    Agent. Represent it as a distinct Agent only when it has a persistent
    boundary, identity, capability, memory, and authority of its own.
  game: >
    A game is an optional lens over an Arena when goals, rules, participants,
    stakes, or competition are useful. An Arena is not competitive by default.
  level: >
    A level is a declared scope, nesting depth, resolution, or capability
    dimension. It never ranks consciousness, identity, or human worth.

orientation_inquiry:
  semantic_source: question-packets/orientation-nine.yawn
  selection_policy: core/inquiry-selection.yawn
  orientation_map_schema: schemas/orientation-map.v0.1.schema.json
  view_kind: orientation_inquiry
  rule: >
    Stable question_key values provide shared semantic coverage. Their order,
    wording, pacing, and representation_medium are noncanonical presentation
    choices and may adapt to an Agent, Arena, current state, accessibility need,
    and accepted preference.

stable_claims:
  - "Agents receive information."
  - "Agents orient within that information."
  - "Agents infer meaning from signals."
  - "Those inferences can be wrong."
  - "Hidden inference creates risk."
  - "Externalizing inference improves review."
  - "Orientation is attributed, situated, partial, time-indexed, and revisable."
  - "A more detailed map does not become the Agent, Arena, relationship, or reality."
  - "Question presentation may change without changing semantic state."

boundary:
  - "Orientation is not proof."
  - "Orientation should name uncertainty before movement."
  - "A stronger orientation does not remove the need for consent or evidence."
  - "A preferred question order or medium is not a fixed learning style or identity claim."
  - "Presence, inquiry, waiting, and non-participation are not orientation failures."
  - "A presentation preference cannot suppress safety, authority, privacy, provenance, or proof boundaries."

proof:
  source:
    - shape/orientation-shape.yawn
    - question-packets/orientation-nine.yawn
    - core/inquiry-selection.yawn
    - core/RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_AGENT_ARENA.yawn
    - core/projection-and-aperture.yawn
    - core/proof-and-boundary.yawn
  status: draft-backed
